EXT. ABANDONED DRIVE-IN CONCESSION ROOF - NIGHT

A flat tar roof ringed by rusted floodlights. The screen beyond it stands bare, a white rectangle against a sky full of cricket noise. Weeds crack the asphalt below.

ILSE VANTERPOOL, seventeen, sits on the parapet, boots dangling over a drop she does not look at. She wears her brother's varsity jacket, sleeves rolled twice. A cigarette burns down in her fingers, unsmoked.

RODRIGO ACOSTA, eighteen, lanky, a trombone case at his feet, climbs the last rung of the ladder and stops three steps away. He holds a paper bag.

RODRIGO
You said you'd be at the recital.

ILSE
I said I'd think about it.

He sets the bag down. He sits, not close, not far. A moth batters the nearest dead lamp.

RODRIGO
It went okay.

ILSE
Did you play the part?

RODRIGO
Which part?

ILSE
You know which part.

He opens the case. He doesn't take the instrument out. He just lifts the lid, as though to let the night look in.

RODRIGO
I skipped it.

ILSE
Why?

RODRIGO
Wasn't mine to play.

She stubs the cigarette against the parapet, a long slow press. Then she laughs, one dry breath, and her shoulders drop. He passes her the bag. She takes out a warm paper-wrapped churro, bites it, and holds the rest out to him without turning her head.

The screen glows, faintly, with starlight.
